A||||10||Richard Parnell||Batboy -swaybar question||||5||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||08-15-2000||06:15 PM||||Batboy- I wonder if you might help on this ques.  What type of rear sway bar should I use on my Fiero w/V8?  Also I am replacing the shocks and struts.  Along with all suspension bushings.  I am planning on using just stock type shocks but am unsure if I should use more beefed up struts for the rear.  What's your opinion?||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||08-15-2000||10:33 PM||||Check to see if JC Whitney has those Addco rear sway bars back in stock yet. Those are probably about as good as you can get for the price. I have a Fiero Store bar, I think it's the same brand, but was more expensive (the only place I could find one for sell at the time). As for the rear struts, I'm just using a good premium gas strut. BUT...the rear springs need to be much stiffer, IF you're still running the original stock springs. I'd recommend a rear coil over kit. Best thing I did to my Batmobile's suspension.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Richard Parnell||08-15-2000||11:24 PM||||Thanks Batboy-  I'll probably do the stiffer springs later.  At least I'll be able to judge the difference then.||||reg||14
Z||000003||Spyder||08-16-2000||11:26 AM||||I’m using the ADDCO (JC Whitney).  <BR>Just make sure you go ahead and use the poly replacement bushings everywhere. <BR>Also make sure the strut that you buy can be modified later to be a coilover. (I speakath from experience, mine wouldn‘t)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||08-16-2000||02:25 PM||||I thought most coil overs came with struts.  Do you usually have to buy the struts separately?  I noticed a site (I forget which one) that has a fairly cheap "coil over" setup but it obviously doesn't come with struts.  I thought about that one, but decided I just couldn't afford it right now.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||08-16-2000||09:01 PM||||Some rear coil-over kits come with a strut preinstalled (like my RCC coil-overs) and others come without the strut so you can use which ever strut you want.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||08-17-2000||01:52 AM||||Thanx, BB.  Which would be cheaper??  Which would be better??||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||08-17-2000||07:39 AM||||Well, my RCC coil-over kit that came with Gabriel gas charged struts and goes on exactly like the stock ones. The advantage of the ones without struts is that you get to choose which struts you want, especially if you want Konis or something super hi-fi like that. I believe you do have to modify the strut (grind a little on it) in order to get them to fit.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 08-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Phil||08-17-2000||10:08 AM||||I used the Ryane setup because I already had new struts . All you have to do is cut off the original welded on spring perch, not a very hard job.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||08-17-2000||10:24 AM||||What's the average cost of the coil overs without struts??  I have a brand new pair of struts (Monroes).||||reg||1
Z||000010||Phil||08-17-2000||01:03 PM||||I believe Ryane was $220||||reg||1
